{
  "statement": "Strict liability for high-risk AI systems is being sold as a safety measure, but look at who is buying. The same large firms lobbying for liability thresholds are the ones who can afford the compliance infrastructure — audits, insurance, legal teams — that small developers cannot. Microsoft's reverse acquihire of Inflection AI's staff for $650 million in March 2024 never triggered antitrust review, but it concentrated talent and modeling capacity. The EU AI Act's compliance costs for SMEs are estimated at 3–5 times their R&D budgets as a share of revenue, compared to less than 1% for Big Tech. That is not safety engineering; that is incumbents using regulation to pull up the drawbridge.\n\nEven if liability is imposed, it will not deter harm because the victims cannot see inside the box. In the 2022 case of a healthcare algorithm that denied cardiac rehab to Black patients, the hospital could not access the vendor's model internals — trade secret claim blocked discovery. The GDPR right to explanation has produced exactly zero successful lawsuits under Article 22 in six years. Strict liability without mandatory transparency and discovery rights is a paper tiger: it creates a legal category of 'high risk' but no way for harmed parties to connect the harm to a specific design choice. The result is performative accountability — press releases about liability frameworks while actual discrimination or medical errors remain unpunished.\n\nThe sequencing here matters. Opponents say transparency can come after liability, but that order lets incumbents shape the transparency rules. They will define 'explainable' as whatever their current audit vendor says, they will set compute thresholds at levels that exclude startups, and they will demand proprietary safeguards as a condition of access. Insurance markets for opaque high-risk AI are nonexistent — no actuary will price a risk they cannot model. So the smaller developer faces a binary choice: pay compliance costs that exceed their runway or exit the market. The large firm pays the same compliance costs as a fraction of revenue and calls it a marketing expense. Who is this regime actually protecting?",
